Z||000009||Jaygee79||01-24-2001||09:58 AM||||I have Fire, theft, & glass (whichever one that is) and last year I paid about $800 for a 10 month plan.  But that's also because I'm 21.  They still consider me a 'new' driver.  I have a clean record too.<P>Oh yeah, that's AFTER the discounts for AAA and driver's ed.<p>[This message has been edited by Jaygee79 (edited 01-24-2001).]||||reg||11
Z||000010||Oreif||01-24-2001||10:09 AM||||Just to note, To insure a Fiero isn't bad. But some insurance companies consider cars labeled as "GT" a sports car. As an example, Although an 86 SE and an 85 GT are basically the same car the SE might be considered a "commuter car" where the GT would be considered a sports car. I'm not sure which ones do this. (I think state farm is one of them) I pay $95.00 every 6-months. (when driving, In storage is $7.50 per 6 months) It is insured as a "collectable classic" and insured for it's apprasied value. (this also makes me exempt from emission testing)||||reg||1
Z||000011||p4n1c||01-24-2001||10:13 AM||||The Fiero GT is cheaper to insure for me than a Cutlass International.  I have no clue why, but the quote for the GT was lower.  But I still remember the insurance agent going "A GT?  Ohhh, the insurance companies love those.".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>"Each passing day, every passing face seems like such a blur" <BR>- The Offspring(Million Miles Away)||||reg||1
